    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d712">

                <pb facs="0733" n="721" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg721"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/10122: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-20T12:00:00+03:00">April 20,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 4:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>263. General Alexander Mazarakis, ex-Minister of War, has formed a new
                    government, composition of which is to be announced today. King retains
                    Presidency of Council, the General being Vice President.</p>
                <p>British Army is now actively preparing withdrawal to Thermopylae line but British
                        Minister<anchor n="4b" xml:id="d712-f1"/> tells me that at a military
                    conference yesterday afternoon General Papagos expressed doubt as to whether
                    Greek Army in Albania can continue its retreats successfully and protect British
                    flank. Mazarakis was inclined to dissent from this opinion and General Wavell
                    said the British forces will not evacuate if Greeks undertake to continue the
                    retirement in liaison with them. He asked King to inform him of Greek decision
                    “within a couple of days”. Should the decision be in the affirmative General
                    Papagos will probably be replaced as Commander-in-Chief. If in the negative
                    British will evacuate their troops as best they can.</p>
                <p>Inform War.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
